<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Isenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1744 CE – 1815 CE · capital Birstein</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hessian principality of the Isenburg-Birstein line that rose to sovereignty under Napoleon only to be mediatised at Vienna.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-isenburg-budingen">Isenburg</a> had held lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a> and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budingen">Büdingen</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, divided into many lines. The Birstein branch was raised to princely rank in 1744 under Wolfgang Ernst I, ruling from Birstein Castle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogelsberg">Vogelsberg</a> foothills.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was small and agrarian, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offenbach-am-main">Offenbach am Main</a> as its most important town, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenot</a> settlers had built crafts and trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-prince-of-isenburg">Prince Charles</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> in 1806, gaining full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and absorbing the lands of other <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-isenburg-budingen">Isenburg</a> lines, and supplied a regiment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> army.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> fall <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-isenburg-budingen">Isenburg</a> was occupied by the allies, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> mediatised it. In 1816 its lands were divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hesse">Electorate of Hesse</a>, the princes keeping their estates.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-isenburg.jpg" alt="Principality of Isenburg" width="306" height="266"><figcaption>Image: Heiliges_Römisches_Reich_1400.png: Gustav Droysen derivative work: Beckstet (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isenburg_1400.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1744 CE – 1815 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Birstein</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wolfgang Ernst I, Charles</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Isenburg-Birstein, Fürstentum Isenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1780&amp;focus=principality-of-isenburg" aria-label="Principality of Isenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4853 424 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Isenburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5173 680L5208 686M5173 680L5229 694M5173 680L5199 691M5173 680L5198 698"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5208 686h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5198 698h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5173 680h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5173 680h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1780&amp;focus=principality-of-isenburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
